  abstract = {Based on his extensive experience on the bench, Justice Cardozo offers an early, highly practical hybrid account of adjudication. He estimates that the vast majority of legal disputes (roughly nine-tenths) are straightforward, requiring judges to mechanically apply clear precedent (the formalist view). However, for the remaining narrow band of 'hard cases' where the law is silent, contradictory, or socially outdated, Cardozo argues that the judge must adopt a realist and creative stance, drawing on sociology, ethics, and custom to construct a fair ruling.}
