  abstract = {Synopsis Background: Property owner and tenant filed action against neighbor for trespass, violation of tree cutting statute, and malicious erection of structure. Neighbor brought counterclaim for private nuisance, trespass, quiet title, intentional infliction of emotional distress, and negligent infliction of emotional distress, and also sought injunctive relief. Following a bench trial, the Superior Court, Judicial District of Litchfield, John D. Moore, J., 2015 WL 1283233, rendered judgment in favor of neighbor on plaintiffs' trespass and tree cutting violations, in favor of plaintiffs on malicious erection of structure, in favor of owner on all of neighbor's counterclaims, in favor of tenant on private nuisance and emotional distress claims, and in favor of neighbor on his claims for trespass and quiet title, enjoined tenant from loitering on or blocking right-of-way, ordered tenant to remove tree platform, enjoined tenant from erecting another structure in its place, and awarded neighbor \$400 in damages. Tenant appealed. Holdings: The Appellate Court held that: [1] evidence supported finding that neighbor entered property to cut tree and build retaining wall with owner's permission, and thus was not liable for trespass or violation of tree-cutting statute; [2] rear and middle sections of neighbor's fence were not maliciously erected structure; [3] front section of fence was maliciously erected structure, and thus removal was warranted; [4] evidence was sufficient to demonstrate that tenant dumped snow onto trees, killing them, as required for neighbor's trespass claim; [5] tenant's tree platform constituted trespass against neighbor; [6] neighbor was entitled to permanent injunction enjoining tenant from blocking right-of-way; [7] harm to neighbor by tenant's conduct did not constitute severe emotional distress, as required for neighbor's intentional and negligent infliction of emotional distress claims; and [8] tenant's conduct was not outrageous, as required for neighbor's intentional infliction of emotional distress claim. Affirmed.},
