  abstract = {Leiter, a leading modern philosopher of Legal Realism, reassesses the historical debates between realists and formalists. He argues that American Legal Realists (like Karl Llewellyn) were never global skeptics who believed all law is completely indeterminate. Instead, they took for granted that the law operates formalistically in easy cases. Realism is defended as a descriptive, local theory specifically designed to explain how judges navigate rationally indeterminate appellate cases where the law fails to dictate a single unique outcome.}
